1
00:00:00,820 --> 00:00:04,580
The next section in the text is secondary hemostasis and related disorders,

2
00:00:04,980 --> 00:00:10,060
and just as we did in the prior section, we first want to cover a little bit of normal before we go into the disorders.

3
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:16,180
Let's remind ourselves that the goal of secondary hemostasis is to stabilize the weak platelet plug,

4
00:00:16,540 --> 00:00:18,500
and that occurs via the coagulation cascade.

5
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:25,840
Remember that when we formed the plug, let's go back and remind ourselves that this is the area of vessel damage.

6
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:28,620
We're going to have von Willebrand's factor coming in,

7
00:00:28,620 --> 00:00:35,320
and then, of course, the platelet is going to adhese via GP1B binding to von Willebrand's factor.

8
00:00:36,380 --> 00:00:41,040
And then beyond that, the next step would be, after release of the mediators,

9
00:00:41,220 --> 00:00:45,540
will be that the platelets will then aggregate, a whole bunch of platelets will aggregate,

10
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:50,480
using the receptor called GP2B3A.

11
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:54,860
And remember that the cross-linking molecule that allows for that aggregation,

12
00:00:54,860 --> 00:00:58,480
all the platelets to join together, this molecule is called, right here,

13
00:00:58,480 --> 00:00:59,520
is called fibrinogen.

14
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:07,840
And fibrinogen is important because it is the area that the coagulation cascade is going to work on.

15
00:01:08,220 --> 00:01:11,000
What I mean by that is this platelet plug is weak,

16
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:15,200
and what happens is the coagulation cascade generates something called thrombin.

17
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:18,820
The end product of the coagulation cascade is the generation of thrombin.

18
00:01:19,180 --> 00:01:22,900
Thrombin comes in and converts fibrinogen to fibrin.

19
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:28,140
When fibrin is produced, fibrin can then be cross-linked to stabilize this interaction,

20
00:01:28,580 --> 00:01:35,740
so that the end product of this entire cascade would then be the production of something called the platelet fibrin thrombus.

21
00:01:35,940 --> 00:01:38,720
Platelet because platelets are the key cell here,

22
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,760
and fibrin because the final cross-linked product is a derivative of fibrin.

23
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:49,540
So this entire thing now becomes a stable platelet fibrin thrombus.

24
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:57,500
And remember that thrombus is just the band-aid that is used to cover the area of disruption of the blood vessel.

25
00:01:57,840 --> 00:02:01,860
And so I say that here in your text by saying that the coagulation cascade generates thrombin.

26
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:05,300
This converts fibrinogen in the platelet plug to fibrin,

27
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:10,280
and then fibrin is then cross-linked, yielding the final platelet fibrin thrombus.

28
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:13,560
Now remember that the factors of the coagulation cascade,

29
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:16,520
they're actually produced by the liver in an inactive state.

30
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:20,700
They float around in the blood until eventually they get activated.

31
00:02:21,440 --> 00:02:23,160
Activation requires a few things.

32
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:25,760
It requires exposure to an activating substance,

33
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:29,640
and I'll talk a little bit more about that in a couple slides.

34
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,300
It requires a phospholipid surface, and I can ask you a question here,

35
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:36,460
so where is the phospholipid surface coming from?

36
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:38,640
The answer is the surface of the platelets.

37
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:42,900
Remember that what this entire reaction is occurring,

38
00:02:43,460 --> 00:02:47,500
basically I'm overdrawing this with these receptors being very big,

39
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:48,780
but the platelets are huge,

40
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:53,520
and this entire interaction is occurring basically on the surface of the platelets.

41
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:56,860
And finally, calcium is also essential,

42
00:02:57,240 --> 00:02:58,980
and calcium is derived from the platelets.

43
00:02:59,060 --> 00:03:02,060
Remember that the dense core granules contain calcium.

44
00:03:02,840 --> 00:03:05,540
Now in disorders of secondary hemostasis,

45
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:08,940
it's usually going to be a problem of factor abnormalities because, of course,

46
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:16,360
the whole process of secondary hemostasis is dependent on all these factors which lead to the eventual production of thrombin.

47
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:24,460
And so the disorders of secondary hemostasis are usually going to be due to problems with factor amount, for example.

48
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:28,800
Now the clinical features of secondary hemostasis are going to be deep bleeding.

49
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:31,380
They get deep tissue bleeding into muscle and joints.

50
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:35,760
Now remember that in primary hemostasis, the key problem was skin and mucosal bleeding,

51
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:38,400
and skin and mucosa are superficial surfaces.

52
00:03:38,880 --> 00:03:41,540
But this is deep tissue bleeding, like into muscles and joints.

53
00:03:41,940 --> 00:03:44,820
And they can also get re-bleeding after surgical procedures.

54
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:48,360
So these are, for example, wisdom tooth extraction, just as an example.

55
00:03:48,360 --> 00:03:55,200
And so these are some of the classic clinical findings in problems of secondary hemostasis.

56
00:03:56,140 --> 00:04:02,880
Now, of course, because we've got disruption of the coagulation factors in these particular disorders,

57
00:04:03,060 --> 00:04:06,940
the most useful laboratory study will be to study the different pathways.

58
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:12,960
And so we can look at the PT, which measures both the extrinsic pathway and the common pathway,

59
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:18,480
and we can measure the PTT, which measures the intrinsic pathway and the common pathway.

60
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:24,100
Now here what I like to do is stop for a moment and just remind you of the normal coagulation pathways.

61
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:28,820
I know that this is one of the biggest headaches for medical students to try to have to memorize this pathway,

62
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:34,440
but it's actually very easy if you just have a couple simple ways of remembering it.

63
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:40,020
So let me just tell you that the goal of the coagulation cascade is to produce the factor X.

64
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:41,820
It's kind of like the old Olympics.

65
00:04:41,820 --> 00:04:46,340
I know they don't do this anymore in the Olympics, but it used to be that you wanted to produce the perfect 10.

66
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:49,840
So the goal of this entire pathway is to produce the perfect 10.

67
00:04:50,140 --> 00:04:52,400
Now on this side, basically we're going to start at 12.

68
00:04:52,780 --> 00:04:57,340
So we'll go 12, 11, skip 10, because we want to make that 9, 8.

69
00:04:58,020 --> 00:05:05,880
All right, 12, 11, skip 10, 9, 8, 10, and then 7, because we're going to 7, 8, 9, skip 10, 11, 12, going the other way.

70
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:10,760
So again, just from left to right, 12, 11, skip 10, 9, 8, 10 is there,

71
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:12,360
and put 7 on the right-hand side.

72
00:05:12,840 --> 00:05:15,120
So this then represents my two separate pathways,

73
00:05:15,560 --> 00:05:19,240
and of course then the generation beneath that will be the common pathway,

74
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:22,340
and so this will be 5, 2, and 1.

75
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:29,980
And the way I remember that is 5 times 2 is 10, or you can say 1 times 2 times 5 is 10.

76
00:05:29,980 --> 00:05:33,180
All right, and so you can work your way up towards the number 10.

77
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:37,540
So 10 becomes a very important number in trying to remember this pathway.

78
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:41,100
Of course, you don't have all the details here, but at least this gets you started.

79
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:43,800
Now a few important things to remember just based on this.

80
00:05:44,040 --> 00:05:45,680
First of all, I can ask you a simple question.

81
00:05:45,860 --> 00:05:48,060
You know, which side, this side or this side has more numbers?

82
00:05:48,120 --> 00:05:51,040
And you clearly see that this left side has more numbers,

83
00:05:51,140 --> 00:05:53,180
and so therefore when we want to measure the left side,

84
00:05:53,180 --> 00:05:55,380
we're going to measure it with the one that has more letters.

85
00:05:55,380 --> 00:06:00,380
So the PTT actually measures this particular side of the pathway,

86
00:06:00,620 --> 00:06:04,620
and the PT measures this particular side of the pathway.

87
00:06:05,580 --> 00:06:10,760
Another important point to recognize is that in the intrinsic pathway,

88
00:06:10,900 --> 00:06:12,380
which is here on the left-hand side,

89
00:06:12,620 --> 00:06:15,480
the key factor that gets activated first is factor 12,

90
00:06:15,740 --> 00:06:19,860
and what activates factor 12 is subendothelial collagen.

91
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:24,180
So subendothelial collagen activates factor 12.

92
00:06:24,580 --> 00:06:26,180
And if you look on the right-hand side,

93
00:06:26,420 --> 00:06:29,640
which this right-hand side represents the extrinsic pathway,

94
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:34,580
then the key thing that activates factor 7 is tissue thromboplastin.

95
00:06:34,920 --> 00:06:37,060
And notice again, this is easy for me to remember

96
00:06:37,060 --> 00:06:38,660
when I don't memorize these things.

97
00:06:38,720 --> 00:06:41,260
I'm just sitting here talking about, again, the top of my head,

98
00:06:41,580 --> 00:06:43,700
and again, I just remember more numbers,

99
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,680
so I use PTT, and PTT has three letters,

100
00:06:47,020 --> 00:06:49,240
so the subendothelial collagen,

101
00:06:49,240 --> 00:06:50,440
which also has three letters,

102
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:52,220
is going to be the activator on this side.

103
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:54,960
And on this side, I remember PT, which has two letters.

104
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:57,600
Well, I know it's PT because it's not the PTT,

105
00:06:58,060 --> 00:06:59,060
and there's two letters there,

106
00:06:59,140 --> 00:07:01,760
so tissue thromboplastin also has two letters.

107
00:07:02,180 --> 00:07:03,700
That's what helps me to remember this.

108
00:07:04,220 --> 00:07:07,160
The other thing to remember is the fact that

109
00:07:07,160 --> 00:07:10,240
which one of these two measurements, PT or PTT,

110
00:07:10,460 --> 00:07:11,940
is better when you give heparin,

111
00:07:11,980 --> 00:07:15,240
and which one is more useful when you give coumadin or warfarin,

112
00:07:15,300 --> 00:07:17,340
which helps to inhibit both of these pathways.

113
00:07:17,340 --> 00:07:19,480
Now, the idea here is remember that

114
00:07:19,480 --> 00:07:22,360
one is better to measure the heparin effect,

115
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:24,360
and one is better to measure the coumadin effect.

116
00:07:24,720 --> 00:07:27,480
And so the way I remember this is that PTT has three letters,

117
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:28,860
and when you walk around on the floors,

118
00:07:28,940 --> 00:07:31,000
you're going to talk about heparin as hep,

119
00:07:31,340 --> 00:07:35,280
and so PTT is the better measure for the heparin effect.

120
00:07:35,340 --> 00:07:37,260
If you want to see what's going on

121
00:07:37,260 --> 00:07:38,920
after you give the patient heparin,

122
00:07:38,980 --> 00:07:41,240
the PTT is a better measure of that.

123
00:07:41,240 --> 00:07:44,120
And therefore, the opposite would be

124
00:07:44,120 --> 00:07:47,440
that the coumadin effect is better measured by the PT.

125
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:49,580
So that's some of the ways that I keep

126
00:07:49,580 --> 00:07:53,380
this crazy different pathways in my mind,

127
00:07:53,460 --> 00:07:54,700
and I hate memorizing,

128
00:07:54,840 --> 00:07:56,660
so I've always got to have some funny way to do it.

129
00:07:57,060 --> 00:07:59,040
Now let's go back and study the first disorder,

130
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:00,340
and that's hemophilia A.

131
00:08:00,520 --> 00:08:03,440
This is a genetic defect in factor VIII.

132
00:08:03,840 --> 00:08:04,160
All right?

133
00:08:04,220 --> 00:08:04,840
That's important.

134
00:08:04,980 --> 00:08:06,720
So you've got a factor VIII deficiency,

135
00:08:06,900 --> 00:08:08,780
and it's funny when I'm sitting with my students,

136
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:13,200
hemophilia, forget about hemophilia A,

137
00:08:13,280 --> 00:08:16,520
let's call it hemophilia VIII, VIII, okay?

138
00:08:16,620 --> 00:08:18,120
So that A sticks in your mind there.

139
00:08:18,480 --> 00:08:20,200
Funny way to do it, but whatever works.

140
00:08:20,580 --> 00:08:22,380
So hemophilia VIII, all right?

141
00:08:22,540 --> 00:08:24,840
This is an X-linked recessive disorder,

142
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:27,500
predominantly affects males because it's X-linked,

143
00:08:27,760 --> 00:08:29,760
and can arise from a new mutation

144
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:30,900
without any family history.

145
00:08:31,080 --> 00:08:33,380
So it's not uncommon in this disorder

146
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:34,600
to get de novo mutations

147
00:08:34,600 --> 00:08:36,000
where you don't have a family history,

148
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,900
but the disorder arises all of a sudden de novo

149
00:08:38,900 --> 00:08:40,640
in one of the offspring.

150
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:42,080
So that's important to note.

151
00:08:42,160 --> 00:08:44,500
You don't need to always have a family history.

152
00:08:44,620 --> 00:08:45,900
That's what I'm trying to highlight here.

153
00:08:46,280 --> 00:08:48,120
Now, these patients have a factor VIII deficiency,

154
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:50,540
so they're going to present with deep tissue joint

155
00:08:50,540 --> 00:08:51,700
and post-surgical bleeding.

156
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:54,480
These are the classic signs and symptoms

157
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:57,860
of patients who have disorders with secondary hemostasis.

158
00:08:58,320 --> 00:08:59,780
The clinical severity will depend

159
00:08:59,780 --> 00:09:00,900
on the degree of deficiency.

160
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:03,320
If there's a heavy deficiency or a major deficiency,

161
00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:05,220
of course, it would be more clinically severe.

162
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:07,900
As far as laboratory findings are concerned,

163
00:09:07,900 --> 00:09:10,020
there's going to be an increase in the PTT

164
00:09:10,020 --> 00:09:11,260
and a normal PT.

165
00:09:11,580 --> 00:09:13,220
So let's go back and remind ourselves,

166
00:09:13,300 --> 00:09:15,740
if we just draw my little coagulation cascade,

167
00:09:15,740 --> 00:09:18,260
we know we start with 12, and then we go to 11,

168
00:09:18,380 --> 00:09:19,380
and we have to skip 10.

169
00:09:19,480 --> 00:09:21,460
So we're going to go to 9, we're going to go to 8,

170
00:09:21,520 --> 00:09:23,300
and then we're going to go to 7 up here,

171
00:09:23,340 --> 00:09:24,620
and we're going to put 10 down here.

172
00:09:24,980 --> 00:09:27,260
So when we're thinking about these two sides,

173
00:09:27,320 --> 00:09:29,300
remember that this left side has more numbers,

174
00:09:29,380 --> 00:09:30,920
so we're going to measure this with the PTT,

175
00:09:30,920 --> 00:09:33,580
and this side, the right side, has fewer numbers,

176
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:35,500
so we're going to measure that with the PT, correct?

177
00:09:36,120 --> 00:09:39,660
And when we think about the fact that in hemophilia A,

178
00:09:39,780 --> 00:09:41,680
we've got an 8 deficiency,

179
00:09:42,120 --> 00:09:43,240
so we're going to knock out 8,

180
00:09:43,280 --> 00:09:45,580
and if we knock out 8, the PTT is going to go up.

181
00:09:45,980 --> 00:09:47,980
In addition, when you have a prolonged PTT,

182
00:09:48,180 --> 00:09:49,880
you're then going to measure the factor 8 levels,

183
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:51,680
and the factor 8 levels will clearly be down.

184
00:09:52,100 --> 00:09:53,940
Now remember that there's no effect on the platelets here,

185
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:55,240
so the platelet count will be normal.

186
00:09:55,240 --> 00:09:57,860
The bleeding time, where it to be measured classically,

187
00:09:58,200 --> 00:09:59,780
would be normal as well.

188
00:09:59,780 --> 00:10:00,940
So what's the treatment?

189
00:10:01,060 --> 00:10:03,820
You simply give back the patient recombinant factor 8,

190
00:10:04,040 --> 00:10:05,800
and of course that makes up for the deficiency.

191
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:08,720
Now in the next disorder, hemophilia B,

192
00:10:08,860 --> 00:10:10,840
all we do is go one step back,

193
00:10:10,900 --> 00:10:13,040
and instead of having a factor 8 deficiency,

194
00:10:13,180 --> 00:10:14,820
we actually have a factor 9 deficiency.

195
00:10:14,980 --> 00:10:17,060
This is also called Christmas disease, by the way.

196
00:10:17,500 --> 00:10:20,200
And so the idea here is you've got 12, 11,

197
00:10:20,820 --> 00:10:24,300
we skip 10, 9, 8, and then we put 7 over here,

198
00:10:24,300 --> 00:10:25,420
and of course 10 is here.

199
00:10:25,740 --> 00:10:27,480
So instead of having a factor 8 deficiency,

200
00:10:27,640 --> 00:10:28,760
we kind of go one step back,

201
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:30,660
and now we've got a factor 9 deficiency,

202
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:32,500
and that's hemophilia B.

203
00:10:33,220 --> 00:10:35,180
All right, so a genetic factor 9 deficiency.

204
00:10:35,360 --> 00:10:38,400
Now clinically, it's going to resemble hemophilia A, right?

205
00:10:38,480 --> 00:10:40,220
Because it's the exact same pathway,

206
00:10:40,340 --> 00:10:41,520
you're just one step above.

207
00:10:42,120 --> 00:10:43,860
So the only difference will be that

208
00:10:43,860 --> 00:10:45,940
instead of having a decrease in factor 8 levels,

209
00:10:46,100 --> 00:10:48,800
you're going to have a decrease in factor 9 levels.

210
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:52,240
So the factor 9 levels would actually be decreased in this disorder.

211
00:10:52,240 --> 00:10:58,620
Another disorder of secondary hemostasis is for a patient to develop a coagulation factor inhibitor.

212
00:10:58,620 --> 00:11:02,380
Now this is when they get an acquired antibody against a coagulation factor,

213
00:11:02,560 --> 00:11:05,640
which then results in impairment of that factor function,

214
00:11:05,640 --> 00:11:08,000
because if you've got an antibody blocking the factor,

215
00:11:08,060 --> 00:11:10,980
the factor can't participate in the coagulation cascade.

216
00:11:10,980 --> 00:11:16,080
And the most common coagulation factor inhibitor is an anti-factor 8.

217
00:11:16,420 --> 00:11:20,060
So it's very similar to hemophilia 8, right?

218
00:11:20,160 --> 00:11:21,140
Or hemophilia A.

219
00:11:21,340 --> 00:11:24,060
Because in hemophilia A, you didn't have factor 8,

220
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:25,880
and in a coagulation factor inhibitor,

221
00:11:25,980 --> 00:11:28,180
you have an antibody against factor 8,

222
00:11:28,260 --> 00:11:29,920
which doesn't let factor 8 function.

223
00:11:30,660 --> 00:11:32,020
Now how are you going to make that distinction?

224
00:11:32,180 --> 00:11:34,000
Well clinically, and on lab findings,

225
00:11:34,540 --> 00:11:37,240
you're going to have the same findings in both hemophilia A

226
00:11:37,240 --> 00:11:40,100
and in a coagulation factor inhibitor.

227
00:11:40,360 --> 00:11:41,960
But basically in both of these cases,

228
00:11:42,300 --> 00:11:45,200
you're going to have an increase in the PTT.

229
00:11:45,860 --> 00:11:47,960
And you're going to have similar clinical findings.

230
00:11:48,320 --> 00:11:49,840
The way by which you're going to make the distinction

231
00:11:49,840 --> 00:11:51,940
is by doing something called a mixing study.

232
00:11:52,040 --> 00:11:54,740
And this becomes very high yield for examination purposes.

233
00:11:55,240 --> 00:11:56,240
When you do a mixing study,

234
00:11:56,320 --> 00:11:58,080
what you do is you take normal plasma

235
00:11:58,080 --> 00:11:59,780
and you mix it with the patient's plasma.

236
00:12:00,200 --> 00:12:02,960
If you take normal plasma and mix it with the patient's plasma

237
00:12:02,960 --> 00:12:04,700
and the patient has hemophilia A,

238
00:12:05,080 --> 00:12:06,380
then by giving the normal plasma,

239
00:12:06,380 --> 00:12:08,020
you're going to give back some factor 8

240
00:12:08,020 --> 00:12:12,560
and that's sufficient enough to reduce the PTT

241
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:14,980
and so to make it go back to normal.

242
00:12:15,380 --> 00:12:17,540
And so you can actually correct the PTT

243
00:12:17,540 --> 00:12:20,080
by mixing in normal plasma

244
00:12:20,080 --> 00:12:24,620
with the patient's plasma in hemophilia A.

245
00:12:25,180 --> 00:12:28,040
However, in the case of a coagulation factor inhibitor,

246
00:12:28,320 --> 00:12:29,960
you're not going to be able to correct it

247
00:12:29,960 --> 00:12:32,740
because as soon as you add the patient's normal plasma,

248
00:12:33,040 --> 00:12:35,100
there's so much antibody against factor 8

249
00:12:35,100 --> 00:12:38,560
that the antibody will, again, bind the normal,

250
00:12:39,140 --> 00:12:41,320
the factor 8 that's coming from the normal plasma

251
00:12:41,320 --> 00:12:43,480
and you won't be able to correct the PTT.

252
00:12:43,960 --> 00:12:45,620
So the mixing study would fail

253
00:12:45,620 --> 00:12:48,100
when there is a coagulation factor inhibitor.

254
00:12:48,260 --> 00:12:52,280
And this is a very high yield distinction to be made here.

255
00:12:52,280 --> 00:12:54,600
So that's important to bear in mind.

256
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:56,960
The next of the disorders that we want to discuss

257
00:12:56,960 --> 00:12:58,160
is von Willebrand's disease

258
00:12:58,160 --> 00:13:01,060
and this is a genetic defect in von Willebrand's factor.

259
00:13:01,460 --> 00:13:04,580
This is the most common inherited coagulation disorder.

260
00:13:04,920 --> 00:13:06,380
So that's very high yield.

261
00:13:06,500 --> 00:13:07,500
It's high yield to know that.

262
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:09,320
It's also high yield to know this disorder.

263
00:13:09,960 --> 00:13:11,120
Multiple subtypes exist.

264
00:13:11,260 --> 00:13:13,300
We're not going to go into that level of detail here,

265
00:13:13,300 --> 00:13:16,800
but we can have qualitative or quantitative defects

266
00:13:16,800 --> 00:13:17,960
with von Willebrand's factor.

267
00:13:18,160 --> 00:13:20,260
We're only going to talk about the most common type.

268
00:13:20,660 --> 00:13:23,060
In the most common type, which is autosomal dominant,

269
00:13:23,260 --> 00:13:26,040
you get decreased levels of von Willebrand's factor.

270
00:13:26,380 --> 00:13:28,600
Now that decreased levels of von Willebrand's factor

271
00:13:28,600 --> 00:13:32,320
is going to result in a problem with platelet adhesion, correct?

272
00:13:32,560 --> 00:13:34,100
Because remember that when you've got

273
00:13:34,100 --> 00:13:37,060
that exposed subendothelial collagen with the initial injury,

274
00:13:37,440 --> 00:13:38,980
you've got von Willebrand's factor,

275
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:41,760
and von Willebrand's factor acts as a linker molecule

276
00:13:41,760 --> 00:13:44,380
for the platelet to adhese using GP1B.

277
00:13:44,740 --> 00:13:46,260
So if you don't have von Willebrand's factor,

278
00:13:46,440 --> 00:13:48,860
that initial step of platelet adhesion can't occur,

279
00:13:49,140 --> 00:13:51,340
which is going to disrupt primary hemostasis.

280
00:13:51,900 --> 00:13:53,260
And so these patients are going to present

281
00:13:53,260 --> 00:13:56,560
with mild mucosal and skin bleeding, right?

282
00:13:56,620 --> 00:13:58,460
These are the signs of primary hemostasis

283
00:13:58,460 --> 00:14:00,260
as we talked about in the prior section.

284
00:14:00,580 --> 00:14:01,840
Again, I've explained why.

285
00:14:02,300 --> 00:14:04,280
What will the laboratory findings be?

286
00:14:04,360 --> 00:14:05,880
Well, there'll be an increased bleeding time

287
00:14:05,880 --> 00:14:06,980
were that to be performed.

288
00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:08,680
And again, that would be because

289
00:14:08,680 --> 00:14:10,780
you don't have good platelet adhesion,

290
00:14:10,780 --> 00:14:12,600
and if you don't have good platelet adhesion,

291
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,120
you're not going to be able to seal off

292
00:14:15,120 --> 00:14:17,040
a damaged blood vessel quickly.

293
00:14:17,460 --> 00:14:18,460
And so instead of bleeding,

294
00:14:18,880 --> 00:14:20,180
the normal bleeding time being

295
00:14:20,180 --> 00:14:21,740
anywhere from two to seven minutes,

296
00:14:21,820 --> 00:14:22,640
it'll be prolonged,

297
00:14:22,780 --> 00:14:24,960
maybe 10 minutes or 15 minutes, for example.

298
00:14:25,760 --> 00:14:27,860
What's also interesting in this particular disorder

299
00:14:27,860 --> 00:14:30,140
is that the PTT is going to be up.

300
00:14:30,600 --> 00:14:32,940
And the reason why the PTT is going to be up

301
00:14:32,940 --> 00:14:34,880
is because you need your von Willebrand's factor

302
00:14:34,880 --> 00:14:36,960
to be able to stabilize factor VIII.

303
00:14:37,120 --> 00:14:39,600
And so that's a high-yield principle to be aware of.

304
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:43,500
Von Willebrand's factor helps in stabilizing factor VIII.

305
00:14:43,860 --> 00:14:45,700
And if you don't have von Willebrand's factor,

306
00:14:45,860 --> 00:14:47,740
you're going to lose stability of factor VIII,

307
00:14:47,960 --> 00:14:49,300
and that's going to increase the PTT.

308
00:14:49,520 --> 00:14:50,800
Now, again, it increases the PTT

309
00:14:50,800 --> 00:14:54,020
because you've got 12, 11, skip 10, 9, 8,

310
00:14:54,140 --> 00:14:55,900
put 7 here, 10 here,

311
00:14:56,240 --> 00:14:57,980
because that's the final goal of both pathways.

312
00:14:58,360 --> 00:15:00,060
And again, if you've got a disruption of VIII,

313
00:15:00,180 --> 00:15:01,540
this has more numbers on this side,

314
00:15:01,600 --> 00:15:03,800
so the PTT is then going to be prolonged.

315
00:15:04,320 --> 00:15:06,600
Now, it so happens that although the PTT is prolonged,

316
00:15:06,600 --> 00:15:09,540
these patients generally don't get problems

317
00:15:09,540 --> 00:15:11,200
with secondary hemostasis.

318
00:15:11,840 --> 00:15:16,820
So there's enough of a lack of stability of factor VIII

319
00:15:16,820 --> 00:15:19,400
that it will change the labs and increase the PTT,

320
00:15:19,880 --> 00:15:22,420
but there is not enough of a lack of stability

321
00:15:22,420 --> 00:15:23,700
to create clinical problems.

322
00:15:23,780 --> 00:15:25,820
So it doesn't lead to clinical problems.

323
00:15:25,920 --> 00:15:28,440
Of course, the PT would be normal.

324
00:15:29,180 --> 00:15:31,340
Perhaps the most important laboratory finding

325
00:15:31,340 --> 00:15:33,300
to be aware of in von Willebrand's disease

326
00:15:33,300 --> 00:15:35,880
is an abnormal Ristocetin test.

327
00:15:36,480 --> 00:15:38,240
The idea here is that if you were to put,

328
00:15:38,380 --> 00:15:39,940
if you were to introduce Ristocetin

329
00:15:39,940 --> 00:15:41,320
to the patient's platelets,

330
00:15:41,800 --> 00:15:43,220
just normal platelets

331
00:15:43,220 --> 00:15:46,160
that have normal von Willebrand's factor as well,

332
00:15:46,440 --> 00:15:48,240
then what would happen is that the Ristocetin

333
00:15:48,240 --> 00:15:50,920
would cause the platelets to all aggregate together.

334
00:15:51,420 --> 00:15:52,480
In this particular case,

335
00:15:52,600 --> 00:15:55,100
because the patient is lacking von Willebrand's factor,

336
00:15:55,220 --> 00:15:57,800
which is necessary for that initial step of adhesion,

337
00:15:58,160 --> 00:15:59,880
you won't be able to have the platelets

338
00:15:59,880 --> 00:16:01,360
all aggregate together.

339
00:16:01,360 --> 00:16:03,980
And so the Ristocetin test will be abnormal,

340
00:16:04,460 --> 00:16:07,240
and that's particularly high yield in this discussion.

341
00:16:07,820 --> 00:16:09,320
Now the treatment is desmopressin.

342
00:16:09,460 --> 00:16:10,560
That's very high yield.

343
00:16:10,700 --> 00:16:11,940
That goes back to pharmacology.

344
00:16:12,140 --> 00:16:13,760
The mechanism is also high yield.

345
00:16:14,100 --> 00:16:15,920
The mechanism by which desmopressin works

346
00:16:15,920 --> 00:16:17,900
is that it increases the release

347
00:16:17,900 --> 00:16:19,420
of von Willebrand's factor

348
00:16:19,420 --> 00:16:21,480
from the Weibol-Pilotti bodies.

349
00:16:21,580 --> 00:16:22,940
Now there's no rocket science here.

350
00:16:22,960 --> 00:16:24,040
I've already explained this to you.

351
00:16:24,380 --> 00:16:26,060
Remember that the Weibol-Pilotti bodies

352
00:16:26,060 --> 00:16:28,740
are the bodies present in the endothelial cells

353
00:16:28,740 --> 00:16:30,740
that you'd see on electron microscopy.

354
00:16:30,740 --> 00:16:33,620
I reminded you several times in the past

355
00:16:33,620 --> 00:16:35,320
that the W reminds me

356
00:16:35,320 --> 00:16:37,540
that they contain von Willebrand's factor,

357
00:16:37,860 --> 00:16:38,980
and the P reminds me

358
00:16:38,980 --> 00:16:41,040
that they also contain P-selectin.

359
00:16:41,080 --> 00:16:42,360
And we talked about P-selectin

360
00:16:42,360 --> 00:16:43,980
in the inflammation chapter.

361
00:16:44,420 --> 00:16:45,420
So basically here,

362
00:16:45,500 --> 00:16:46,320
all you have to remember

363
00:16:46,320 --> 00:16:48,540
is that desmopressin induces release

364
00:16:48,540 --> 00:16:50,220
of von Willebrand's factor

365
00:16:50,220 --> 00:16:51,820
from the Weibol-Pilotti bodies.

366
00:16:52,560 --> 00:16:54,860
Another problem of secondary hemostasis

367
00:16:54,860 --> 00:16:56,660
can arise with vitamin K deficiency.

368
00:16:56,660 --> 00:16:59,180
Remember that vitamin K is necessary

369
00:16:59,180 --> 00:17:04,500
for gamma-carboxylation of factors 2, 7, 9, 10, C, and S.

370
00:17:04,580 --> 00:17:05,960
This is something you have to burn in your brain.

371
00:17:06,040 --> 00:17:07,260
It shows up on every exam.

372
00:17:07,820 --> 00:17:09,360
So you need to be able

373
00:17:09,360 --> 00:17:14,180
to gamma-carboxylate factors 2, 7, 9, 10, C, S.

374
00:17:14,640 --> 00:17:16,660
And vitamin K is essential

375
00:17:16,660 --> 00:17:19,460
for being able to gamma-carboxylate those factors.

376
00:17:19,900 --> 00:17:21,120
And remember that vitamin K

377
00:17:21,120 --> 00:17:23,780
is activated by epoxide reductase in the liver.

378
00:17:23,780 --> 00:17:26,280
So vitamin K comes in through the gut.

379
00:17:26,800 --> 00:17:27,680
It goes to the liver.

380
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,900
It gets activated by epoxide reductase.

381
00:17:31,340 --> 00:17:32,540
Once it's activated,

382
00:17:32,740 --> 00:17:37,260
it can then gamma-carboxylate factors 2, 7, 9, 10, C, and S.

383
00:17:37,600 --> 00:17:39,440
And just as a farm reminder,

384
00:17:39,560 --> 00:17:40,440
remember that Coumadin,

385
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:42,360
the way by which it works

386
00:17:42,360 --> 00:17:45,540
is that it actually blocks epoxide reductase.

387
00:17:45,620 --> 00:17:47,240
If epoxide reductase is blocked,

388
00:17:47,560 --> 00:17:48,900
vitamin K can't be activated.

389
00:17:49,040 --> 00:17:50,580
And if vitamin K can't be activated,

390
00:17:50,940 --> 00:17:53,360
then factors 2, 7, 9, 10, C, and S.

391
00:17:53,360 --> 00:17:55,400
can't be gamma-carboxylated

392
00:17:55,400 --> 00:17:56,920
and hence won't function properly.

393
00:17:57,560 --> 00:17:58,660
Now vitamin K deficiency

394
00:17:58,660 --> 00:17:59,820
is a little bit interesting

395
00:17:59,820 --> 00:18:01,500
and goes back to a little bit of biochemistry.

396
00:18:01,920 --> 00:18:03,000
Remember that vitamin K

397
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,500
is essentially generated by the bacteria in our gut.

398
00:18:06,980 --> 00:18:08,980
And so the patients who get deficient

399
00:18:08,980 --> 00:18:11,320
are going to have some sort of abnormality

400
00:18:11,320 --> 00:18:12,840
of either the bacteria in the gut

401
00:18:12,840 --> 00:18:14,920
or the ability to take up vitamin K.

402
00:18:15,240 --> 00:18:17,080
For example, newborns,

403
00:18:17,460 --> 00:18:21,200
their gut is not yet colonized by bacteria.

404
00:18:21,200 --> 00:18:24,020
And so because they have a lack of GI colonization,

405
00:18:24,620 --> 00:18:26,360
they are particularly susceptible

406
00:18:26,360 --> 00:18:30,960
to getting problems of vitamin K deficiency-related bleeding.

407
00:18:31,440 --> 00:18:32,560
And that's why at birth,

408
00:18:32,680 --> 00:18:35,300
one of the injections that we give a baby,

409
00:18:35,400 --> 00:18:36,160
a newborn at birth,

410
00:18:36,240 --> 00:18:37,980
is we prophylactically give all newborns

411
00:18:37,980 --> 00:18:39,260
when they're born vitamin K.

412
00:18:39,680 --> 00:18:40,680
We give them vitamin K

413
00:18:40,680 --> 00:18:42,660
to avoid hemorrhagic disease of the newborn.

414
00:18:43,080 --> 00:18:44,000
So that's one example.

415
00:18:44,420 --> 00:18:46,960
Another example would be long-term antibiotic therapy.

416
00:18:47,320 --> 00:18:48,060
The idea here,

417
00:18:48,060 --> 00:18:49,800
if you have long-term antibiotic therapy,

418
00:18:49,940 --> 00:18:53,460
you're killing off the gut flora.

419
00:18:53,740 --> 00:18:54,940
And as you kill off the gut flora,

420
00:18:55,060 --> 00:18:57,320
again, you're not going to be able to generate vitamin K.

421
00:18:57,800 --> 00:18:59,300
And then finally, malabsorption.

422
00:18:59,380 --> 00:19:01,580
Remember that vitamin K is a fat-soluble vitamin.

423
00:19:01,760 --> 00:19:03,560
And so if you don't have good absorption,

424
00:19:03,720 --> 00:19:05,860
you won't be taking up your fat-soluble vitamins,

425
00:19:06,060 --> 00:19:06,660
for example.

426
00:19:07,060 --> 00:19:10,100
And so vitamin K, vitamin A, E, D,

427
00:19:10,360 --> 00:19:11,200
these are the other ones,

428
00:19:11,300 --> 00:19:12,400
A, E, D, and K

429
00:19:12,400 --> 00:19:14,800
are the four key fat-soluble vitamins.

430
00:19:14,800 --> 00:19:18,020
And so malabsorption could result in a vitamin K deficiency,

431
00:19:18,400 --> 00:19:20,280
especially if there's fat malabsorption.

432
00:19:20,820 --> 00:19:23,180
Finally, liver failure can also cause

433
00:19:23,180 --> 00:19:26,700
a secondary hemostatic problem.

434
00:19:27,240 --> 00:19:28,420
And the reason why is because

435
00:19:28,420 --> 00:19:30,220
the vast majority of the coagulation factors

436
00:19:30,220 --> 00:19:30,940
are made in the liver.

437
00:19:31,100 --> 00:19:32,020
That's the first reason.

438
00:19:32,360 --> 00:19:34,940
The second reason why is because epoxide reductase,

439
00:19:35,060 --> 00:19:36,640
which was what activated vitamin K,

440
00:19:36,920 --> 00:19:38,360
is also present in the liver.

441
00:19:38,520 --> 00:19:39,480
So that would fail.

442
00:19:39,780 --> 00:19:41,340
So basically, when you get liver failure,

443
00:19:41,340 --> 00:19:43,260
there's two reasons why you're going to get

444
00:19:43,260 --> 00:19:44,880
secondary hemostatic problems.

445
00:19:45,020 --> 00:19:46,480
One, you're not producing factors.

446
00:19:46,700 --> 00:19:48,720
And two, even if you're producing factors,

447
00:19:48,980 --> 00:19:51,220
they're not getting secondarily activated

448
00:19:51,220 --> 00:19:55,860
because epoxide reductase can't activate vitamin K.

449
00:19:56,160 --> 00:19:58,300
And then if you don't have that activation of vitamin K,

450
00:19:58,640 --> 00:20:01,700
then 2, 7, 9, 10, C, and S won't function properly.

451
00:20:02,260 --> 00:20:03,500
Now, when you have liver failure,

452
00:20:03,820 --> 00:20:05,640
it's important to remember that the key way

453
00:20:05,640 --> 00:20:07,620
by which you measure the effect of liver failure

454
00:20:07,620 --> 00:20:09,780
on coagulation is using the PT.

455
00:20:09,780 --> 00:20:12,380
So the PT is the way by which you follow

456
00:20:12,380 --> 00:20:16,500
the effect of liver failure on coagulation.

457
00:20:17,160 --> 00:20:19,240
Large-volume transfusions are the very last thing

458
00:20:19,240 --> 00:20:20,160
that we're going to discuss.

459
00:20:20,460 --> 00:20:21,860
When you give a large-volume transfusion,

460
00:20:21,960 --> 00:20:23,960
you're diluting out the coagulation factors

461
00:20:23,960 --> 00:20:25,740
until you get a relative deficiency

462
00:20:25,740 --> 00:20:27,000
of the coagulation factors,

463
00:20:27,160 --> 00:20:31,040
which can also cause a disorder of secondary hemostasis.

464
00:20:31,540 --> 00:20:32,960
So that closes off our section.

465
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:33,940
Again, not complicated.

466
00:20:34,260 --> 00:20:35,780
It's most important to remember the normal.

467
00:20:35,980 --> 00:20:37,660
Once you remember the normal,

468
00:20:37,660 --> 00:20:39,740
then there's a few very simple principles

469
00:20:39,740 --> 00:20:41,880
to derive from that,

470
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:44,880
which again then will help you to understand

471
00:20:44,880 --> 00:20:46,720
most of the disorders in this section.

